Y6 Press 'Return' when you've finished reading: 
 
This game simulates the struggle between the Good, represented by you, and the
Evil, represented by Sauron, The dark Lord of the Ring.
The time is 12 April, 3019 TA. You have the thing that Sauron wants: A Ring
that he made some 3200 years back. This Ring is the Lord of Rings! It has the
power of making the wearer invisible and sharpen his senses (except seeing). If
Sauron gets that, he will become so powerful that he will dominate all of
Middle Earth for a Very Long period of time!
 
The only way of victory against Sauron is to throw the Ring into the volcano
where it once was made. That volcano, Orodruin, Mt Doom, lies in the middle of
Mordor, Saurons Country. To your disposal are several Men, Elves, Hobbits and
Dwarwes. Sauron on the other hand has hords of Orcs, Men and Trolls. He also
has 9 Nazgul (Ringwraiths) who are slaves under Rings given to them by Sauron
at the end of the Second Age.
Y6 Press 'Return' when you've finished reading: 
There also is a "thing" belonging to a race ancestral to the Hobbits. That
"thing" is Gollum, Sm`agol, who strangled his bosom friend, D`agol, who found
the Ring in Anduin, The Great River, where Isildur had dropped it (which lead
to his untimely death) in TA 2, fleeing from a band of Orcs. Sm`agol then began
to hate light, and crept into the Misty Mountains, where he lived in about 2500
years, till one day a very unfortunate Hobbit, Bilbo Baggins, found his way
through Orc-tunnels and on the floor found The Ruling Ring! Sm`agol began to
hate Bagginses and some years later crept out of the mountain in hunt for the
Ring. He is now constantly out to get the one that has got his "preciouss".
There also is Shelob, a giant spider-like monster that guards the back-way to
Mordor. She always wants meat!
Y6 Press 'Return' when you've finished reading: 
Now you may wonder what happened to Bilbo. He got out of all his misadventures.
(If you want to know more about them, read J.R.R. Tolkiens "The Hobbit")
Later, he gave the Ring to his nephew, Frodo, one of our heroes, and
dissappeared from the tales.
 
 
